<header><p class="st-kicker">sultanate · Horn and East Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Sultanate of the Geledi</h1><p class="st-meta">1700 CE – 1910 CE (approx.) · capital Afgooye</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Somali sultanate of the fertile lower Shabelle valley that became the strongest power in southern Somalia in the 19th century.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In the early 18th century the Geledi clan, led by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gobroon-dynasty">Gobroon dynasty</a>, rose to power at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afgooye">Afgooye</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shebelle-river">Shabelle</a> river after the fall of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/ajuran-sultanate">Ajuran Sultanate</a>. Its founder <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibrahim-adeer">Ibrahim Adeer</a> is remembered for overthrowing local rivals and establishing the dynasty's religious prestige.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The sultans controlled rich farmland where grain and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> were grown, partly by slave labour, and exported produce through <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/sultanate-of-mogadishu">Mogadishu</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marka">Merca</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/barawa">Barawa</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yusuf-mahamud-ibrahim">Yusuf Mahamud Ibrahim</a> made the Geledi the leading power of the south, attacking the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baardheere">Bardera</a> jihadists in 1843. Islamic learning flourished, and the sultans held annual ceremonies at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afgooye">Afgooye</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmed-yusuf-of-geledi">Ahmed Yusuf</a> was killed fighting the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bimaal">Bimaal</a> in 1878, and the sultanate declined. In 1908 the Geledi accepted Italian authority, and the sultanate was absorbed into <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/italian-somaliland">Italian Somaliland</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gobroon-dynasty">Gobroon</a> line surviving as a traditional house.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/sultanate-of-the-geledi.jpg" alt="Sultanate of the Geledi" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Sultanatemaker · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Geledi_Sultanante_Flag.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1700 CE – 1910 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>sultanate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Afgooye</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Ibrahim Adeer, Yusuf Mahamud Ibrahim, Ahmed Yusuf</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Somali (Maay)</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/somalia">Somalia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Geledi Sultanate, Gobroon dynasty, Geledi</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1805&amp;focus=sultanate-of-the-geledi" aria-label="Sultanate of the Geledi on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5933 2098 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Sultanate of the Geledi lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6253 2354L6202 2325M6253 2354L6259 2357"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6202 2325h0M6259 2357h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6253 2354h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6253 2354h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Afgooye. <a href="/atlas?year=1805&amp;focus=sultanate-of-the-geledi">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
